Finding: {"title":"Audit-log POST endpoint lets any admin caller forge performedByUserId/performedByName","severity":"MEDIUM","area":"Audit integrity / attribution spoofing","files":["src/server/routes/routes-ai-schedule.ts"],"detail":"POST /locations/:locationId/ai-schedule/audit-log (routes-ai-schedule.ts:3458-3504) takes `performedByUserId` and `performedByName` directly from the request body (lines 3474-3475) instead of deriving them from `authUser` (which is already available via `c.get('authUser')`). Every other audit-log write in this file (schedule_published, schedule_generated) hardcodes 'system'/'AI Scheduler', so this endpoint is the one place a client fully controls attribution.","impact":"Any admin (or anything with an admin token) can write an audit-log entry claiming a different user performed an action, undermining the audit trail's evidentiary value.","recommendation":"Derive performedByUserId/performedByName from `authUser` server-side; if the client needs to attribute to a different subject (e.g. an instructor acting on a student's behalf), use `subjectStudentId`/`subjectStudentName` for that, not the performer fields.","confidence":"high"}
confirmed=true only if the defect genuinely exists. If misread → verdict="false-positive". If real but wrong severity → set correctedSeverity. If real but underspecified → verdict="needs-detail" (kept). Return JSON per the schema.

